Sport asks the upper limb for things normal life does not: throwing, falling, pushing, holding. The injuries are different, and so must be the recovery.

I follow athletes of every level, from the amateur who wants to get back on the bike to the person who competes. The method is the same: understand the movement, repair what is needed, build the return.

# Shoulder instability

> In young people, after the first episode, the risk of recurrence is very high.

The typical injury of contact sports and sports with falls (rugby, football, skiing, motorcycling). Arthroscopic stabilisation — Bankart, with remplissage when there is a Hill-Sachs lesion — has a low recurrence rate when well indicated and followed by serious rehabilitation.

# Overuse injuries

> Here surgery is the exception.

In overhead sports (volleyball, tennis, swimming, throwing) the shoulder wears through repetition: cuff tendinopathy, superior labral tears, internal impingement. The work is on technique, load and kinetic chain, with the physiotherapist and the coach.

# Fractures

> Sometimes fixing a fracture shortens the time.

Clavicle, humerus, wrist, scaphoid. In athletes the choice between a cast and surgery also considers the calendar, and allows safe training to resume earlier.

# Return to sport

I do not give a date: I give three criteria.

{% affiancati %}
{% colonna titolo="Motion" %}
Full and symmetric.
{% /colonna %}
{% colonna titolo="Strength" %}
Recovered, and measured.
{% /colonna %}
{% colonna titolo="Movement" %}
The sport-specific movement, without pain and without apprehension.
{% /colonna %}
{% /affiancati %}

# When you go back

> When all three are there, you go back.

The programme is written and shared with your coach.
